# What the root Dockerfile is allowed to see. This file decides what gets *uploaded*, not
# only what gets copied: `azure.yaml` sets `remoteBuild: true`, so every `azd deploy mcp`
# tars the working tree and sends it to Azure Container Registry. Without this file that
# upload is ~250 MB — a 73 MB .venv, a 176 MB GTFS export and the collected lake — for an
# image that needs about 2.5 MB of it.
#
# Deny everything, then re-include exactly what the Dockerfile COPYs. The allow-list is
# the safer direction: a new directory (a lake snapshot, a downloaded dataset, an .env
# written during a demo) is excluded by default instead of silently joining the upload,
# and this repository is public — anything that reaches the registry has left the laptop.
# If a build ever fails with "file not found", the fix is one `!line` here, and that is a
# better failure than a quarter-gigabyte push nobody notices.
*

# The wheel build inputs. LICENSE and NOTICE.md are inputs too, not decoration:
# pyproject.toml names them in `license-files`, and hatchling fails a build that cannot
# find them.
!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!LICENSE
!NOTICE.md
!src

# The reference layer: the gazetteer, the occupancy profile the collector built, and GTFS.
!data/reference

# Byte-code caches, wherever they are. They are rebuilt in the image anyway and copying
# them in risks shipping a .pyc whose .py has since changed.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]

# The three GTFS tables the server never opens. stop_times.txt is 150 MB, stop_times.csv
# 26 MB and trips.csv 5.8 MB; all three are inputs to `make sequences`, whose output —
# route_sequences.json.gz, 127 KB — is what ibb_mcp.gtfs.load_stop_sequences actually
# reads. Leaving them out is not only 97% of the upload: it also means a request inside a
# scale-to-zero container can never fall into build_stop_sequences and start parsing a
# 150 MB file, because the file is not there to parse.
data/reference/gtfs/stop_times.txt
data/reference/gtfs/stop_times.csv
data/reference/gtfs/trips.csv
